	- Also, the staged/working-file effects depend on what differences exist between B and C.

	And if your specific goal is "I accidentally did git reset ... HEAD~1 and want to go back to where I was", use:

	git reflog                  → Shows the history of previous HEAD positions and reference updates, including actions such as commits, resets, checkouts, merges, and rebases 
                                → Which can be used to find commits that are no longer reachable from the current branch.
							    → Important: git reflog is primarily a local recovery mechanism. Reflog entries are not normally shared with GitHub or other teammates.

	✨ After git reflog you will see like this:
		a82f91d HEAD@{0}: reset: moving to HEAD~1       → current position
		7c21e44 HEAD@{1}: commit: Add login feature     → previous HEAD position
		91ab234 HEAD@{2}: commit: Add user API          → position before that

	git reset --<mode> <previous-HEAD-commit>                 → Moves HEAD back to the previously recorded commit using the selected reset mode.
	git reset --soft 7c21e44 OR git reset --soft HEAD@{1}     → That is the proper way to recover from a reset.

	✨ git reflog can help recover commits after soft, mixed, or hard resets, as long as the relevant commit is still present/reachable through the local reflog and hasn't been garbage-collected.
			 → For --soft and --mixed, your changes are still present in the working/staging area, so recovery is generally straightforward.
			 → For --hard: the changes from C are removed from your working tree. Reflog can recover the commit itself, but recovering uncommitted changes that were never committed is a different matter.

	✨ So remember: Reflog recovery
			--soft   → ✅ Yes
			--mixed  → ✅ Yes
			--hard   → ✅ Yes, if the commit is still recoverable

	
	6️⃣ COMMON STEPS FOR TEAM COLLABORATION
	────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
	

		1. Team Leader creates the GitHub repository        	   → Create the main repository with the initial project files.
		2. Team Leader adds all teammates as Collaborators  	   → Give team members access to the repository.

		3. All teammates clone the repository                      → Each teammate gets a local copy of the project.
		4. [VERY IMPORTANT] Create your own branch           	   → NEVER do your feature development directly on `main`.
		5. Work only on your own branch                      	   → Write and modify code related to your assigned task.
		6. Complete your work and check the changes          	   → Use git status and test your code.
		7. Stage and commit your changes                     	   → git add . and git commit -m "Add login functionality"
		8. Push your branch to GitHub                        	   → git push -u origin <your-branch-name>
		9. Inform your teammates                             	   → Tell the team that your work is complete and your branch is pushed.

		10. Team reviews the changes                               → Review the code/Pull Request before merging it into `main`.
		11. Merge the branch into `main`                     	   → Merge the feature branch into `main` only after the changes are reviewed and approved.



		# 1️⃣ On GitHub: Open Pull Request → Review → Approve → Click "Merge pull request"
		# 2️⃣ From command line using `pull`:
				git switch main           → Switch to the `main` branch.
				git pull origin main      → Fetch the latest changes from the remote repository `origin/main` branch and merge (integrate) them into the local `main` branch.
				git merge feature-login   → merge (integrate) the `feature-login` branch into the current `main` branch.
																	
		# 3️⃣ From command line using `fetch`:
				git switch main           → Switch to the `main` branch.
				git fetch origin          → Download the latest commits and remote-tracking information from the remote repository `origin/main`. It does NOT modify the current branch or working files.
				git merge origin/main     → merge (integrate) the latest fetched `origin/main` into the local `main` branch.
				git merge feature-login   → merge (integrate) the `feature-login` branch into the current `main` branch.

		# The key difference between pull and fetch
				git pull origin main      → Fetch + integrate remote main
				git fetch origin          → Fetch only → Updates origin/main → Does NOT change your current branch
				git merge origin/main     → Integrates the fetched remote main into your current branch

		🔥 So remember:
				git fetch  → "Download the latest information from the remote repository, but do not change my current branch."
				git merge  → "merge (integrate) the specified changes into my current branch."
				git pull   → "Fetch the latest changes and merge (integrate) them into my current branch.
				git stash  → Used to temporarily save your uncommitted changes so that you can get a clean working directory without committing those changes.

		✨ Stashing:
				git stash          → Temporarily save uncommitted changes.
				git stash list     → Show all saved stash entries.
				git stash apply    → Restore stashed changes without removing the stash entry.
				git stash pop      → Restore stashed changes and remove the stash entry if the apply succeeds.
				git stash drop     → Delete a specific stash entry.
				git stash clear    → Delete all stash entries.
				
				git stash push -m "message"   → Stash changes with a descriptive message.
				git stash apply stash@{1}     → Apply a specific stash without removing it.
				git stash pop stash@{1}       → Apply a specific stash and remove it if the apply succeeds.
				git stash -u                  → Stash both tracked and untracked files.

		Important: By default, git stash saves changes to tracked files (both staged and unstaged). 
		Untracked files are not normally included; use git stash -u / git stash --include-untracked when you also want to stash untracked files.

