	🔥 Git Merge Strategy - A merge strategy that determines how Git combines two or more branch histories during a merge.
	─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

		1️⃣ ort        (git merge feature)                         → Modern Git uses ort as the default strategy for normal two-branch merges. If both branches changed the same part of a file, Git may report a merge conflict that you must resolve manually.
		2️⃣ recursive  (git merge -s recursive feature)            → Older default strategy for two-head merges used in older Git versions. In modern Git, ort replaced it as the default. 
		3️⃣ octopus    (git merge feature1 feature2 feature3)      → Merges 3 or more branches at once; generally for merges that can be completed without conflicts. If conflicts are required, Git generally stops rather than performing an octopus merge.  
		4️⃣ ours       (git merge -s ours feature)                 → This creates a merge commit that records that feature was merged, but keeps the current branch's tree/content and ignores the other branch's content.
		5️⃣ subtree    (git merge -s subtree feature)              → Useful when the other repository/tree is related as a subtree, allowing Git to adjust the trees before merging.



