	🔥 Merge-conflict states shown by git status --short 
	─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
		UU - Both sides modified        (Modified  ↔ Modified)      → The file was modified on both the current branch (ours) and the branch being merged (theirs).
		AA - Both sides added           (Added     ↔ Added)         → The file was added on both the current branch (ours) and the branch being merged (theirs).
		DD - Both sides deleted         (Deleted   ↔ Deleted)       → The file was deleted on both the current branch (ours) and the branch being merged (theirs).
		UD - Us modified, them deleted  (Modified  ↔ Deleted)       → The file was modified on the current branch (ours), but deleted on the branch being merged (theirs).
		DU - Us deleted, them modified  (Deleted   ↔ Modified)      → The file was deleted on the current branch (ours), but modified on the branch being merged (theirs).
		AU - Us added, them modified    (Added     ↔ Modified)      → The file was added on the current branch (ours), but modified on the branch being merged (theirs).
		UA - Us modified, them added    (Modified  ↔ Added)         → The file was modified on the current branch (ours), but added on the branch being merged (theirs).

		Merge started       → ❌ Merge not completed
		Conflict occurs     → ❌ Merge not completed
		Resolve conflict    → ❌ Still not completed
		git merge --abort   → Cancel an ongoing merge, even if some conflicts have already been resolved/staged and return to the pre-merge state → Restores the repository to the state before the merge started.
		git add             → ✅ Conflict marked as resolved → Can still use git merge --abort
		git commit          → ✅ Merge completed             → git merge --abort no longer applies



